exp_24: evaluator cross-check overturns half the verdict -- we do NOT beat reference under the real harness

Instrument B (official eval.py, one process per rank, all three arms check: pass,
no parser refusals) disagrees with instrument A on ARM ORDERING, which is the one
thing it was built to test:

                 A (graded)   B (evaluator)   B/A
  ours              339.3         578.7      1.71x
  reference         382.8         509.9      1.33x
  rank-1            309.3         358.1      1.16x

  ours/rank1       1.0971        1.6159
  ours/reference   0.8863        1.1349

The inflation is arm-dependent rather than a shared constant, so it does not cancel
in the ratios. Ordering disagrees on four of six shapes; A and B agree only on
shapes 5 and 6, the two largest, where kernel time dominates per-call overhead.

Reading: instrument A's one-process/8-device shortcut is not a neutral
reparametrization and it favours our kernel specifically. A launches eight devices
from one process sharing one launch path and one IPC setup; the evaluator pays that
cost eight times. Ours inflates most, rank-1 least. That is exp_12's per-call host
tax appearing as a first-order term instead of a footnote, and it moves that item up
the optimization queue: under the protocol that actually grades us it is the largest
single term separating us from rank-1.

Neither instrument is discarded. A is the controlled one -- same-run interleaved,
five arms including a null, full rotation, both protocols, a measured per-shape noise
floor -- and is the right tool for attributing a difference to the kernel. B is the
harness that grades us and is the right tool for stating where we stand. They answer
different questions and must not be blended; A's ours/reference win must not be
quoted as an evaluator result.

Confidence is medium-high on direction, medium on magnitude: the flip is a 28-point
swing, consistent across four shapes and monotone in shape size, but instrument B
ran a single rotation with no repeat to bound drift. A second rotation with reversed
arm order would settle it.

rank-1's warm pass came out 0.97x its bench pass, confirming the repaired driver
warms the JIT correctly and that the mandatory warm -> test -> bench order works.
